   </div>
</header>
  <main class="container">
<div class="aeo-authority-block">
    <a href="/districts/nyc-district-23-brownsville/">← Back to District 23 Hub</a>
</div>

<div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 23 Leadership Directory</h1>
    <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District District 23.</p>

    <div class="contact-grid">

        <div class="contact-box">
            <h3>CSE 5 (Serving District 23)</h3>
            <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>1665 St. Marks Ave, Brooklyn, NY 11233</div>
            <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:718-240-3557">718-240-3557</a></div>
            <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
        <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office.
    </div>


    <section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
        </h2>

        <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

            <div class="protocol-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>.
                </p>
                <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary.</li>
                    <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]</em></li>
                    <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates and names.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="protocol-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder:
                </p>
                <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call.</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal).</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent.</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="important-info" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Important Information for District 23 Families
        </h2>

        <div class="info-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 30px;">
            <div class="info-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Initial Referrals & Evaluations: A Step-by-Step Guide</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">Understanding the initial referral process is crucial. Here's a detailed breakdown:</p>
                <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Step 1: The Written Referral.</strong> Per NYS Part 200.4(a), the process begins with a written referral to the CSE. This can come from a parent, teacher, doctor, or other professional. Your letter MUST clearly state your concerns and the specific reasons you believe your child needs special education services. Include any supporting documentation (medical reports, previous evaluations, schoolwork samples).</li>
                    <li><strong>Step 2: Acknowledgement and Consent.</strong> The CSE must acknowledge your referral in writing. They will then send you a consent form for evaluations. Under IDEA (Individuals with Disabilities Education Act), 34 CFR §300.301, you have the right to an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense if you disagree with the school's evaluation results, provided certain conditions are met.</li>
                    <li><strong>Step 3: Evaluations.</strong> The CSE must conduct a comprehensive evaluation of your child within 60 school days of receiving your consent (NYS Part 200.4(b)(1)). This includes psychological, educational, and, if necessary, medical, social, and other evaluations. Ensure that all areas of suspected disability are assessed. Advocate for specific tests if you believe they are necessary to accurately assess your child.</li>
                    <li><strong>Step 4: The Eligibility Meeting.</strong> After the evaluations, the CSE will hold a meeting to determine if your child is eligible for special education services. You are a critical member of this team. Prepare to discuss the evaluation results and your observations of your child's strengths and weaknesses.</li>
                    <li><strong>Step 5: IEP Development (If Eligible).</strong> If your child is found eligible, the CSE will develop an Individualized Education Program (IEP). This is a legally binding document that outlines your child's specific needs, goals, and services.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

            <div class="info-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Common Pitfalls & How to Avoid Them in District 23</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">Navigating the special education system can be challenging. Here are some common pitfalls in District 23 and how to proactively address them:</p>
                <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Pitfall 1: Delays in Evaluations.</strong> Some parents report delays in the evaluation process exceeding the 60-day timeframe. <strong>Solution:</strong> Document EVERYTHING. Send a certified letter to the CSE requesting confirmation of receipt of your referral and a timeline for evaluations. If the timeline is not met, file a State Complaint with NYSED (New York State Education Department).</li>
                    <li><strong>Pitfall 2: Inadequate IEP Goals.</strong> IEP goals should be Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-bound (SMART). Vague goals are unenforceable. <strong>Solution:</strong> Before the IEP meeting, prepare your own draft IEP goals based on your child's needs and evaluation results. Bring them to the meeting and advocate for their inclusion. </li>
                    <li><strong>Pitfall 3: Lack of Parent Involvement.</strong> Parents are crucial members of the CSE team. Your input is essential. <strong>Solution:</strong> Actively participate in all meetings, ask questions, and voice your concerns. If you feel your concerns are not being heard, request a recess of the meeting to gather more information or seek outside consultation. Consider bringing an advocate to the IEP meeting.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pitfall 4: Resistance to Specific Services.</strong> Districts sometimes resist providing specific services (e.g., 1:1 aide, specialized reading program) due to cost concerns. <strong>Solution:</strong> Focus on documenting your child's *need* for the service. Provide evidence from evaluations, progress reports, and your own observations. Cite relevant research supporting the effectiveness of the requested service. Remember, the IEP must be based on the child's *individual needs*, not the district's budget.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pitfall 5: Inappropriate Placement Recommendations.</strong> The IEP team must consider the least restrictive environment (LRE) for your child. <strong>Solution:</strong> Research different placement options (ICT, SETSS, self-contained, specialized schools) and advocate for the placement that best meets your child's needs while maximizing their interaction with general education peers. Know your rights under IDEA regarding LRE.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)
        </h2>

        <div class="faq-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 20px;">

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">Q: What is the difference between a CSE and a CPSE?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #666;"><strong>A:</strong> The Committee on Special Education (CSE) serves students ages 5-21. The Committee on Preschool Special Education (CPSE) serves children ages 3-5. If your child is turning 5 and receiving services through the CPSE, their case will transition to the CSE.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">Q: What is an IEE, and how do I request one?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #666;"><strong>A:</strong> An Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) is an evaluation conducted by a qualified professional who is *not* employed by the school district. You have the right to request an IEE at public expense if you disagree with the results of the school's evaluation. To request an IEE, you must notify the CSE in writing. The district must either grant your request or initiate a due process hearing to defend their evaluation. (34 CFR §300.502). Make sure your written request clearly states that you are requesting an IEE at public expense.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">Q: What if I disagree with the IEP?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #666;"><strong>A:</strong> You have several options if you disagree with the IEP. First, you can request another IEP meeting to further discuss your concerns. You can also file a written objection to the IEP, which the district must address. If you are still not satisfied, you can pursue mediation or file a due process complaint with NYSED. Filing a due process complaint initiates a formal legal process where an impartial hearing officer will make a decision. (34 CFR §300.507)</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">Q: My child is being bullied. What are the school's responsibilities?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #666;"><strong>A:</strong> Schools have a responsibility to address bullying, especially when it affects a student's ability to access their education. If your child with a disability is being bullied, the school must take steps to investigate and address the bullying behavior. The IEP team may need to modify the IEP to address the bullying, such as providing counseling, social skills training, or a change in placement. Document all instances of bullying and report them to the school principal and the CSE. If the school fails to adequately address the bullying, you may have grounds for a complaint under Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">Q: What are Related Services?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #666;"><strong>A:</strong> Related services are support services that are required to assist a child with a disability to benefit from special education. These services can include, but are not limited to: speech-language pathology, audiology services, psychological services, physical therapy, occupational therapy, counseling services, transportation, and medical services for diagnostic or evaluation purposes. (34 CFR §300.34). If your child needs a specific related service to access their education, it must be included in their IEP.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

</div>
</main>
